1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,105
Good evening from the OROS closing desk.

2
00:00:03,105 --> 00:00:09,005
This morning the system named no pick, so tonight there is nothing to grade.

3
00:00:09,005 --> 00:00:13,740
No setup, no trade, no loss — that is the discipline working.

4
00:00:13,740 --> 00:00:18,676
Why we grade in public: a forecast that is never checked is just content.

5
00:00:18,676 --> 00:00:24,085
Every morning call gets an evening verdict, and both go in the permanent record.

6
00:00:24,050 --> 00:00:28,007
On the day, the book is up 490 dollars.

7
00:00:28,007 --> 00:00:36,833
State Street SPDR S&P Oil & Gas Exploration & Production ETF (XOP) leads, +3.2 percent.

8
00:00:36,833 --> 00:00:42,108
Direxion Daily AAPL Bull 2X ETF (AAPU) lags at -1.0.

9
00:00:42,108 --> 00:00:47,586
The checklist is clear: nothing requires action today.

10
00:00:47,586 --> 00:00:51,113
On screen, the day's gainers and laggards inside the paper book.

11
00:00:51,069 --> 00:00:59,539
And here is the book itself — the actual holdings, identical to this morning's emailed report.

12
00:00:59,539 --> 00:01:09,090
12 positions, led by MetLife, ticker MET, Avnet, ticker AVT, Direxion Daily AAPL Bull 2X ETF, ticker AAPU.

13
00:01:09,090 --> 00:01:21,345
Every line carries its share count, its entry, and its exit — because a position without a defined exit is not a position, it is a hope.

14
00:01:21,301 --> 00:01:26,061
Now the part that makes this system different: the loop.

15
00:01:26,061 --> 00:01:28,441
Every morning OROS proposes.

16
00:01:28,441 --> 00:01:38,896
Every evening it grades its own call, journals the verdict, and adjusts its own risk before it is allowed to propose again.

17
00:01:38,896 --> 00:01:42,806
Inside the book today: 9 positions up, 3 down.

18
00:01:42,806 --> 00:01:45,186
Best, CHRD at +3.82 percent.

19
00:01:45,186 --> 00:01:46,971
Worst, AAPU at -1.03.

20
00:01:46,971 --> 00:01:51,901
Both go in the journal — wins and losses get the same ink.

21
00:01:51,901 --> 00:01:58,956
The honest scoreboard reads: expectancy -4.95% per trade over 340 graded positions.

22
00:01:58,956 --> 00:02:05,926
Because that record is soft, the system has throttled itself to 25% position size.

23
00:02:05,926 --> 00:02:11,026
Nobody told it to — full size must be earned back with wins.

24
00:02:11,026 --> 00:02:26,581
What tonight's read hands to tomorrow's open: 211 names close the day in Stage 2; 1 new invalidation queue exit reviews; position size stays cut to 25% until the record earns it back.

25
00:02:26,549 --> 00:02:28,783
Over to the sports desk.

26
00:02:28,783 --> 00:02:37,996
Day 52 of the race: three books, the same one hundred thousand simulated dollars, since July sixth.

27
00:02:37,996 --> 00:02:42,835
In first place, the index fund, in gray, at 100,813.

28
00:02:42,835 --> 00:02:49,629
Then the live self-rebuilding book, in blue, 1.3 thousand back at 99,529.

29
00:02:49,629 --> 00:02:56,795
Then the frozen first portfolio, the green line, 3.6 thousand back at 97,228.

30
00:02:56,795 --> 00:02:59,401
The race has no finish line.

31
00:02:59,401 --> 00:03:03,682
Same books, same dollars, back at it tomorrow.

32
00:03:03,682 --> 00:03:08,521
Every line starts the same day with the same dollar.

33
00:03:08,521 --> 00:03:10,382
Nothing is restated.

34
00:03:10,382 --> 00:03:12,712
Today's lesson is benchmarking.

35
00:03:12,712 --> 00:03:19,173
Any strategy must beat the boring alternative: the same money parked in an index fund.

36
00:03:19,173 --> 00:03:22,630
That is why the S and P line shares the chart.

37
00:03:22,630 --> 00:03:31,646
If the system cannot outrun it, the index deserves the money, and publishing the race every day keeps the system honest.

38
00:03:31,605 --> 00:03:38,940
All figures are simulated paper trading, graded and journaled by the OROS agents with no human intervention.

39
00:03:38,940 --> 00:03:41,182
Nothing here is financial advice.

40
00:03:41,182 --> 00:03:44,170
From the closing desk — see you at the open.
